{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields/reader-packet-1-part3.json'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields/reader-packet-1-part3.json'","aggregated_output":"{\n \"articles\": [\n  {\n   \"article\": 2,\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia’s Immense Dividend Hike Puts Dividend Growth Stocks in the Spotlight\",\n   \"publisher\": \"morningstar.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"text\": \"Is Nvidia a True Dividend Growth Stock?\\nWhat’s the Big Deal About Dividend Growth Anyway?\\nWhy Are Dividend Growth Stocks Performing Well in 2026?\\nWhat Does Nvidia Joining the Dividend Growth Party Mean?\\nNvidia’s Immense Dividend Hike Puts Dividend Growth Stocks in the Spotlight\\nCompanies growing their dividends have posted strong returns this year. Why is that?\\nLost in a torrent of news about Nvidia NVDA this year—its role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments , its forecasts of 70% year-on-year revenue growth , and its acquisition of Hugging Face —was a dividend announcement. In May, Nvidia told shareholders that it would increase its quarterly payout to $0.25 per share from $0.01 per share. That’s a 2,400% increase, according to my calculations!\\nAnalysts will be quick to point out that perspective is key. “Nvidia’s dividend is virtually immaterial relative to its financial health and forward prospects,” wrote Brian Colello , who covers semiconductor stocks for Morningstar. Nvidia’s dividend yield will continue to register well below 1%. And even more of the company’s cash is being spent buying back shares.\\nBut in absolute terms, Nvidia’s dividend is huge. Something like $25 billion per year will be returned to shareholders. That sum will make Nvidia one of the largest dividend payers in the US stock market.\\nThe Nvidia news also comes at a time when the Morningstar US Dividend Growth Index is neck and neck with the broad US stock market, which is unusual in an up year. So, what’s going on with dividend growth stocks this year? And what does Nvidia’s dividend hike suggest, if anything?\\nDespite the dividend increase, it’s probably too soon to call Nvidia a dividend growth stock. Although the company initiated a quarterly dividend back in 2013, the payout was paltry until the 2026 increase. The Morningstar US Dividend Growth Index requires five consecutive years of dividend growth. Other dividend indexes on the market require an even longer history. Active managers running dividend growth strategies interpret their mandates in different ways.\\n“Most of Nvidia’s distributions to shareholders come in the form of share repurchases,” writes Colello. That’s consistent with the overall US stock market trend. As I wrote about recently , companies have spent far more buying back their own shares than they have on dividends.\\nIndeed, Nvidia announced an $80 billion share buyback program at the time of its dividend declaration. The company’s preference when it comes to cash allocations is clear in the exhibit below.\\nSource: Nvidia 10-K and 10-Q statements reconciled with Morningstar Indexes data by analyst Aryan Singh. Data as of July 26, 2026. Trailing 12-month data used. Buybacks depicted on a gross basis. Download CSV .\\nDividend growth has long been viewed as a symbol of corporate “quality.” Only profitable companies secure in their financial positions consistently increase cash returned to shareholders. While share buybacks can be opportunistic, dividends are a commitment. The market typically punishes dividend cutters.\\nThe nearly 400 stocks in the Morningstar US Dividend Growth Index include a mix of market leaders and less familiar names. Mega-caps like Microsoft MSFT , JPMorgan Chase JPM , Johnson & Johnson JNJ , and ExxonMobil XOM , which sit atop the index because constituents are weighted by market capitalization, are joined by mid- and small-cap stocks like Becton Dickinson BDX , DTE Energy DTE , and American Financial Group AFG .\\nInvestment style-wise, the dividend growth index leans toward value. Technology stocks, which tend to carry higher-than-average multiples, represent just 17.6% of index weight. That’s less than half their share of the broad US stock market. Meanwhile, the generally lower-priced financial services, healthcare, industrials, and consumer defensive sectors are overrepresented among dividend growers.\\nWhat about performance? Dividend growers have a reputation for being defensive stocks. Strong balance sheets and moderate price tags mean that dividend growers often shine during risk-off market environments.\\nIndeed, the Morningstar US Dividend Growth Index has generally outperformed during selloffs. It declined by less than the broad US stock market in down years 2018 and 2022. It also held its value relatively well during the tariff turmoil of March-April 2025 and in negative months like March and June 2026. From a volatility perspective, the dividend growth index has registered a standard deviation of returns well below the market.\\nGiven its defensive profile, it’s somewhat surprising that the dividend growth index is performing well in 2026. After all, the market is up double digits as the fourth quarter approaches. The technology sector has led this year, powered by the AI buildout. Those conditions aren’t typically favorable to dividend growers.\\nThe energy sector helps explain dividend growth’s strong returns. With the conflict in the Middle East pushing up oil prices, energy companies are booking huge profits. Key index constituents include ExxonMobil, with a near four-decade-long history of dividend growth, and ConocoPhillips COP , which rebuilt its dividend growth track record after low oil prices forced a dividend cut in 2016.\\nBut the biggest contributor to 2026 performance—both in absolute and relative terms—is the healthcare sector. Johnson & Johnson, Merck MRK , and UnitedHealth UNH are all having stellar years. J&J is a paragon of dividend growth going back to the 1960s. Merck and UnitedHealth have more modest histories in the neighborhood of 15 years, but UnitedHealth has posted double-digit dividend growth rates.\\nThe strong year for dividend growth reflects a number of trends. First, market leadership broadened from the narrow group of Magnificent Seven companies that dominated 2023-25. Nvidia, for example, has performed well, but not at the same level as in recent years. Amazon.com AMZN , Alphabet GOOGL , and Meta Platforms META have lagged. Second, this has been a year in which value stocks have outperformed growth. That includes the energy and healthcare sectors, but also value technology stocks benefiting from investments. Third, it has generally been a strong year for companies returning cash to shareholders, as I wrote about recently .\\nActually, this isn’t the first time dividend growers have outperformed in an up market. The dividend growth index also posted stellar returns in 2021, another year of market “breadth.” All US sectors posted strong returns then, and little separated growth and value stocks.\\nSome might see Nvidia’s dividend hike as a sign the company is entering a new, more slow-growth life stage. That doesn’t fit with the 70% revenue growth forecast. One thing that’s certain is that Nvidia’s cash pile is massive.\\nNvidia is also part of a trend I’ve written about regarding tech and tech-adjacent companies paying more dividends. Microsoft and Apple AAPL have both been constituents of the dividend growth index for years. In 2024, Meta and Alphabet both initiated payouts to shareholders.\\nNvidia also fits the dividend growth profile when it comes to profitability and financial strength. Increasing a payout can be seen as a sign of corporate quality. Because of their defensive characteristics, dividend growers are a good option for investors concerned about a market bubble .\\nMorningstar, Inc., licenses indexes to financial institutions as the tracking indexes for investable products, such as exchange-traded funds, sponsored by the financial institution. The license fee for such use is paid by the sponsoring financial institution based mainly on the total assets of the investable product. A list of ETFs that track a Morningstar index is available via the Capabilities section at indexes.morningstar.com. A list of other investable products linked to a Morningstar index is available upon request. Morningstar, Inc., does not market, sell, or make any representations regarding the advisability of investing in any investable product that tracks a Morningstar index.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"figure\": 1,\n     \"text\": \"about $25 billion a year returned through the dividend\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 2,\n     \"text\": \"the 2,400% increase of the dividend\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 3,\n     \"text\": \"the $0.25 quarterly dividend\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 3,\n   \"title\": \"Sandisk vs. NVIDIA: Which AI Stock Is the Better Buy Now?\",\n   \"publisher\": \"zacks.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"text\": \"Lately, surging artificial intelligence (AI) and data center demand has benefited both NVIDIA Corporation NVDA and the much smaller Sandisk Corporation SNDK. While the two companies offer different ways to capitalize on the AI boom, which one stands out to be the better buy now? Let's explore –\\nSandisk's AI-Driven Growth Boosts Revenues and Profitability\\nTotal revenues for Sandisk reached $8.97 billion in the fiscal fourth quarter of 2026, up a whopping 372% year over year and 51% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 5 press release.\\nFor fiscal year 2026, revenues reached $20.25 billion, representing 175% year-over-year growth. The Data Center segment has been the major growth driver, with revenues jumping 437% as AI and data center demand increased the need for high-value storage.\\nIn the fourth fiscal quarter, revenue growth came more from higher pricing than higher volumes. As a result, gross margins rose to 84.6% compared to 26.2% a year ago.\\nFor fiscal year 2026, gross margin was 71.5% compared to 30.1% a year earlier. Profitability also improved dramatically, with GAAP net income coming in at $11.43 billion versus a loss of $1.64 billion in the previous year.\\nThe growth doesn't seem to be temporary, as management remains bullish on their near-term outlook. They expect revenues to be between $10.3 billion and $10.8 billion in the fiscal first quarter of 2027, which would mean another strong quarterly growth following a huge fiscal fourth quarter. Now that the company has signed five more New Business Model agreements, revenue growth and profitability will surely improve.\\nNVIDIA's AI Demand Drives Strong Growth and Margin Expansion\\nThe Data Center segment remains NVIDIA's primary growth engine, with revenues jumping 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the Aug. 26 press release. Consolidated revenues soared to $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially.\\nNVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin increased to 75% in the fiscal second quarter compared with 72.5% a year ago. Strong operating performance helped NVIDIA convert robust revenue growth into solid earnings growth.\\nNVIDIA further expects profitability to remain strong and projects revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027. At the midpoint, this would represent a 12% sequential rise. Additionally, the next-generation Vera Rubin platform has entered the full production stage, positioning NVIDIA to benefit from the next phase of AI infrastructure investment.\\nBoth Sandisk and NVIDIA have delivered exceptional earnings growth, fueled by an increase in AI and data center demand, resulting in sharp revenue gains and substantial margin expansion. Both companies also look quite confident about their near-term outlooks.\\nHowever, Sandisk is in a highly cyclical memory business. If supply catches up with demand, memory prices may come under pressure and weigh on margins. Moreover, the company's fast-paced growth has raised expectations, which has made its growth trajectory susceptible if pricing momentum weakens.\\nSince Sandisk's business is dependent on storage demand, it has created greater concentration risk. In contrast, NVIDIA is capitalizing on the broader AI ecosystem, has dominant data center exposure, and continues to deliver exceptional AI-driven growth. To top it off, NVIDIA's 63.7% net profit margin compared with Sandisk's 56.5%, indicates its greater efficiency in converting revenues into bottom-line profits.\\nTaken together, these factors make NVIDIA a better buy than Sandisk at the moment. NVIDIA currently has a Zacks Rank #1 (Strong Buy), while Sandisk has a Zacks Rank #3 (Hold). You can see the complete list of today's Zacks Rank #1 stocks here.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [],\n   \"open_questions\": null\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 4,\n   \"title\": \"Dear Nvidia Stock Fans, Mark Your Calendars for September 24\",\n   \"publisher\": \"barchart.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia (NVDA) investors have a key date to watch as CEO Jensen Huang is set to attend President Donald Trump's state dinner for Chinese President Xi Jinping on Sept. 24, along with other prominent technology leaders at the White House. The high-profile gathering comes as the U.S. and China navigate sensitive issues involving artificial intelligence (AI), trade, tariffs, and advanced semiconductor technology.\\nHuang's presence is particularly notable for Nvidia, given the company's position at the center of the global AI chip market and the ongoing U.S.-China technology relationship. Investors will be watching whether discussions around AI and semiconductor trade create any signals relevant to Nvidia's access to the Chinese market, export restrictions, or the broader outlook for AI infrastructure demand.\\nIntel vs. TSMC: One Chip Stock Controls 72.5% of the Foundry Market. The Other Is Mounting a Comeback.\\nWhile the dinner itself does not guarantee any policy changes, Sept. 24 could provide an important backdrop for Nvidia stock as the market weighs the potential implications of renewed U.S.-China engagement.\\nNvidia is a global leader in accelerated computing and AI, renowned for pioneering the GPU that revolutionized gaming, data centers, and AI-driven computing. Headquartered in Santa Clara, California, Nvidia's technology now powers everything from high-performance gaming and cloud computing to autonomous vehicles and generative AI applications. With a market cap of $5.5 trillion, Nvidia stands among the world's most valuable companies, driven by its dominance in AI infrastructure and continued innovation in next-generation chip design.\\nNvidia stock has gained steady momentum in recent sessions, with the shares now sitting just 4% below their 52-week high of $236.54 reached on May 14. The stock is up 22% year-to-date (YTD) and 28% over the past 52 weeks, while its five-day gain stands at 7%, reflecting an acceleration in recent trading. Although in the red this morning, Nvidia has otherwise advanced in each of the past five sessions through Sept. 22, including gains of 2.5% on Sept. 17, 1.3% on Sept. 18, 2.3% on Sept. 21, and 0.66% yesterday, Sept. 22. It surged marginally in the last session, closing at $228.87.\\nNvidia's recent stock advance has been supported by several developments reinforcing the strength of the AI infrastructure cycle. The company's latest quarterly results showed revenue growth of 106%, while management has continued to signal strong demand for its next-generation AI systems.\\nMeanwhile, demand from AI cloud providers remains robust as Nebius Group (NBIS) announced another increase in prices for Nvidia GPU rentals, while CoreWeave (CRWV) said it was continuing to contract new compute capacity at higher prices, providing fresh evidence that demand for Nvidia-powered computing remains strong. The stock is also benefiting from renewed optimism around the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin platforms as customers continue expanding AI infrastructure.\\nIn addition, expectations surrounding the upcoming Trump-Xi engagement have added another potential catalyst, with investors watching for any developments that could improve Nvidia's ability to sell AI chips in China.\\nNVDA trades at 24.04 times forward earnings and 24.81 times sales, which is currently a premium compared to industry peers.\\nNvidia delivered another blockbuster quarter, reporting its fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, for the quarter ended July 26. Revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year (YoY) from $46.7 billion, and increased 18% sequentially. The result also comfortably exceeded Nvidia's prior guidance of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\\nThe company's profitability expanded even faster. On a non-GAAP basis, net income climbed 118% to $54 billion, versus $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, while adjusted EPS jumped 120% to $2.22, exceeding expectations. Non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%. Operating income surged 124% YoY to $63.7 billion.\\nNvidia's Data Center segment remained the primary growth engine, generating $89 billion in revenue, up 117% YoY from the prior-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue. The performance reflects continued demand for Nvidia's accelerated-computing platforms as hyperscalers and AI companies expand infrastructure.\\nFurthermore, Nvidia issued an even stronger outlook. For Q3 fiscal 2027, the company expects revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. Importantly, the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China. Nvidia expects GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points.\\nIt's worth noting that Nvidia is not merely maintaining its AI growth trajectory; it is accelerating it, with management pointing to full production of its next-generation Vera Rubin platform and continued strength across AI infrastructure.\\nStreet expects Nvidia's momentum to continue, with analysts forecasting EPS growth of 102.4% YoY to $9.25 in fiscal 2027, followed by another 65.7% increase to $15.33 in fiscal 2028.\\nBernstein analyst Stacy Rasgon reiterated a \\\"Buy\\\" rating on NVDA stock this week while maintaining a $400 price target. The target was unchanged from Rasgon's earlier revision, when Bernstein raised it from $315 to $400 on Aug. 27, underscoring the analyst's continued confidence in Nvidia's earnings and AI-driven growth outlook.\\nFurthermore, Rothschild & Co Redburn analyst Timm Schulze-Melander maintained a \\\"Buy\\\" rating on Nvidia with a $325 price target.\\nOverall, NVDA stock has a consensus \\\"Strong Buy\\\" rating. Of the 50 analysts covering it, 46 advise a \\\"Strong Buy,\\\" three suggest a \\\"Moderate Buy,\\\" and one offers a \\\"Strong Sell\\\" rating.\\nThe average analyst price target for NVDA is $325.88, indicating a potential upside of 45%. The Street-high target price of $515 suggests that the stock could rally as much as 129%.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n     \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 5,\n   \"title\": \"I've Held Nvidia for Nearly 3 Years. Here's Whether I'd Still Buy Today.\",\n   \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA) has been one of the best stocks to own in the artificial intelligence (AI) arms race. It is up nearly 1,500% since 2023 began, providing investors with incredible returns. Unfortunately, I do not have those returns in my portfolio.\\nI bought the stock at the beginning of 2023 and watched it double. Then I sold the stock because I was worried that AI computing demand wouldn't last. I was very wrong. I had to disregard all of the prior gains and purchase shares in 2024 after it had run up some more, which has still been a great investment for me.\\nBut would I buy the stock again today? You bet I would.\\nNvidia is still a dominant force in the industry\\nNvidia is turning the investment world on its head. Normally, the law of large numbers dictates that the larger a company is, the harder it is to grow. Nvidia is the world's largest company and has reached heights that no other company has ever achieved. However, it's growing faster than any other stock among the world's top 10 companies. That's hard to believe, but it showcases just how massive the demand is for AI computing power.\\nNvidia makes GPUs , which are the go-to computing unit for all sorts of AI operations, be it training or inference. While there are alternatives, the Nvidia ecosystem cannot be beaten. Additionally, custom AI chips are starting to become more popular among many AI hyperscalers. Still, there will always be a place for computing units like Nvidia's GPUs that are flexible and can handle a wide variety of workloads.\\nDue to Nvidia's market position, it has advanced knowledge of how much computing power the AI hyperscalers are planning. The AI companies are making multiyear plans to ensure they have mapped out when they will need computing units, because data centers don't go up overnight. This gives Nvidia the ability to issue long-term guidance, and the guidance regarding the AI build-out is simply incredible.\\nNvidia estimates that global data center capital expenditures will rise to between $3 trillion and $4 trillion by 2030. Considering the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data centers this year, that's huge growth still to come.\\nSo, if you think you've missed the boat on Nvidia, think again. There is still plenty of upside to come, and Nvidia will be one of the biggest beneficiaries of the AI build-out. However, none of it is priced into Nvidia's stock.\\nThat's pretty much in line with where other big tech stocks trade when one-time effects don't skew their valuations, but there's one problem: Nvidia is growing far faster than any of its big tech peers. The trailing price-to-earnings ratio doesn't account for Nvidia's massive growth ahead, and that's where a forward-looking metric helps out.\\nAt less than 15 times next year's earnings, Nvidia's stock has almost no growth priced into it today. That means the stock has major upside over the next year. Furthermore, with the AI build-out expected to intensify through 2030, that leaves plenty more room for Nvidia to run.\\nEven if you've missed out on Nvidia so far, now is the perfect time to buy more. Very little growth has been priced in, and Nvidia's stock looks well positioned to be one of the best investments over the next few years.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [],\n   \"open_questions\": null\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 6,\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Set For 40% Surge: 3 Catalysts That Could Drive the Next Big Move\",\n   \"publisher\": \"finance.yahoo.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"text\": \"NVDA earns a BUY with a $310.20 target (~40% upside), driven by the Vera Rubin ramp and $500 billion in mobilized AI infrastructure capital.\\nAMD's trailing P/E of 211 dwarfs NVDA's 45, while AVGO's AI semiconductor revenue surged 221%, confirming the AI infrastructure wave underpinning NVDA's outlook.\\nMy 24/7 Wall St. price target for NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA ) is $310.20, which implies 39.56% upside from the current $225.35 quote. I rate NVDA a buy with high conviction.\\nThree catalysts drive the call: the Vera Rubin production ramp, more than $500 billion in third-party AI infrastructure capital being mobilized through NVIDIA partners, and the opening chapter of physical AI across robotics and autonomous vehicles.\\nNVDA is up 21.11% year to date and 28.17% over the trailing year. Q2 fiscal 2027 delivered revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%. Data Center revenue hit $89.023 billion. Management guided Q3 to $108 billion.\\nWhy Bulls See $353 or Higher\\nThe bull-case one-year target is $353.54. Jensen Huang told investors NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand, with fiscal 2028 growth pegged at approximately 70%. Vera Rubin ramps platform value per gigawatt from Blackwell's $25 billion to $40 billion.\\nHyperscaler capex is expected to reach nearly 800 billion in 2026 and 1.3 trillion in 2027, a wave that also pulls in the power, cooling, and networking suppliers we profiled in a free report on seven AI infrastructure names that aren't chipmakers. Analyst sentiment shows 57 Buy or Strong Buy ratings against 2 Holds and 1 Sell.\\nThe bear scenario bottoms at $261.39, still above current price. Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\\nQ3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue citing geopolitical uncertainty. Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\\nAdvanced Micro Devices ( NASDAQ:AMD ) posted Q2 fiscal 2026 revenue of $11.536 billion, up 50.11%, with Data Center up 107%. AMD trades at a trailing P/E of 211 versus NVDA's 45, making NVDA the cheaper name on growth per valuation dollar.\\nBroadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ) competes in custom AI ASICs and networking. Q3 fiscal 2026 revenue reached $29.591 billion, up 85.5%, with AI semiconductor revenue of $16.70 billion growing 221% year over year. Its acceleration validates NVDA's guided $108.0 billion quarter sits inside a real industry wave. The peer set makes $310.20 look reasonable.\\nI'd own NVDA at $225.35 with a 24/7 Wall St. price target of $310.20 and a buy rating at 90% confidence. Supply-constrained demand into a Vera Rubin ramp management calls the fastest in company history drives the call.\\nI'd stay a buyer if hyperscaler capex tracks toward 1.3 trillion in 2027. I'd step aside if memory pricing pushes gross margin below 71% or if China restrictions widen beyond the Data Center compute carve-out.\\nThese projections assume NVIDIA executes on the Vera Rubin ramp and hyperscaler AI spending holds. Significant upside or downside could result from broader China market access, a major AI capex reset, or accelerated adoption of physical AI and robotics platforms.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"figure\": 1,\n     \"text\": \"fiscal 2028 growth of approximately 70%\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 2,\n     \"text\": \"NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n     \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n     \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 7,\n   \"title\": \"What Does NVIDIA Offer That Texas Instruments Does Not?\",\n   \"publisher\": \"trefis.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-25\",\n   \"text\": \"What Does NVIDIA Offer That Texas Instruments Does Not?\\nTexas Instruments (TXN) and NVIDIA both sell chips for the data centers now being built. Data centers bring in nearly all of NVIDIA’s revenue, while TI sells to industrial and automotive customers as well as data centers. You pay 38.2 times the yearly profit for TI, yet only 28.1 times for NVIDIA. Their latest earnings calls show the two businesses parting ways on supply.\\nNVIDIA’s data center revenue was $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total. Management guided fiscal Q3 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. That midpoint is about 12% above the Q2 total. Management expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028.\\nTI guided Q3 2026 revenue to $5.65 billion to $6.15 billion, against $5.5 billion in Q2. TI’s data center sales doubled from a year earlier in Q2. Management said most of the Q3 growth will come from selling more units, not from higher prices. TI also has clean room space it can equip if demand keeps rising.\\nThe two outlooks are different kinds of news. Demand for NVIDIA’s chips is larger than NVIDIA’s supply, so supply sets the pace. TI has spare room to make more, so demand sets TI’s pace.\\nEach company pays for its position. Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations. Gross margin is the share of sales left after production costs. NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\\nNVIDIA has also invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs. Management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA’s balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA’s business in fiscal 2028. That part of NVIDIA’s business would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\\nTI’s cost is its factories. TI spent $3.3 billion on capital projects in the year to Q2 2026. TI’s $8.7 billion of cash from operations covered that more than twice over. TI’s free cash flow over the same period included $1.6 billion of CHIPS Act incentives. The yearly numbers on growth, margin, and debt show how far apart the two already are.\\nThis comparison scores six standard measures of price, growth, profit and debt , and NVIDIA leads Texas Instruments on all six. NVIDIA’s revenue grew 83% over the past year, against 16.7% for TI. NVIDIA kept 65.2 cents of each sales dollar as operating profit, against 38.1 cents at TI.\\nDebt equals 0.7% of NVIDIA’s market value, against 6.1% for TI. NVIDIA carries almost no debt against its value.\\nTI wins on one measure outside those six: price to sales. You pay 11.9 times the yearly revenue for TI, against 17.9 times for NVIDIA. The six scored measures judge price on profit, and on profit NVIDIA is cheaper.\\nTI also has a long dividend record. In September, TI raised the quarterly dividend to $1.52 a share, from $1.42. That was TI’s 23rd straight year of dividend increases.\\nWhat Would Count As A Stumble For NVIDIA?\\nNVIDIA’s lead is wide enough that it would take a real stumble to shrink it. Revenue below NVIDIA’s own guided range for fiscal Q3 would be the first sign of one. NVIDIA holds its call on that quarter on November 17. A result inside or above the range would leave NVIDIA’s lead in place.\\nNVIDIA leads on growth, margins, debt, and price on profit. TI’s strengths are a lower price on sales, spare capacity, and a long dividend record. Revenue below NVIDIA’s guided range in November is the first result that would test that lead.\\nHow To Act On TXN?\",\n   \"named_figures\": [],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n     \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n     \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 8,\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Would Have to Add Almost a Whole Broadcom to Reach $7 Trillion\",\n   \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-25\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) , the world's most valuable company, is worth around $5.45 trillion as of this writing. The next big round number is $7 trillion. And the gap between them is larger than it sounds: roughly $1.55 trillion, or nearly the full market value of Broadcom ( AVGO +2.83% ) , the $1.69 trillion chip and infrastructure software business.\\nWhat would have to be true of Nvidia's revenue, its margins, and its price-to-earnings multiple for the company to add almost a whole Broadcom to its value? And how long could it take?\\nAfter all, fewer than 10 other companies in the world are worth more than the gap itself. At Nvidia's size, even a modest-sounding percentage gain today matches an entire giant company.\\nStep back, though, and the climb looks smaller than the dollars suggest. From around $225 as I write this, a $7 trillion market cap comes out to around $290 a share -- a rise of around 28%.\\nFor context, shares have climbed 37% from their 52-week low, and they now trade within around 5% of their 52-week high. I'd say nothing about a 28% move is unusual for this stock.\\nNvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\\n\\\"Now, compute is revenue,\\\" said CEO Jensen Huang in the company's second-quarter earnings release.\\nThe forecasts go far past the quarter. Guidance for the fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue , around 12% above fiscal Q2. And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \\\"a supply-constrained outlook.\\\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.\\nAnalysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion. Grow that by roughly 70%, and fiscal 2028 lands somewhere around $700 billion.\\nMargins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\\nWhen might it happen?\\nNvidia's stock costs around 14 times forward earnings today, using analysts' fiscal 2028 estimates. At $290 per share, that forward price-to-earnings multiple would sit closer to 18 -- around what Broadcom gets today on the same basis.\\nFiscal 2028 ends in January 2028, roughly 16 months from now. If Nvidia delivers the year it has forecast, and the market prices those earnings the way it prices Broadcom's today, $7 trillion arrives somewhere on that timeline.\\nThe stock could also get there without any increase in its price-to-earnings multiple. Hold that multiple of expected earnings flat, and shares hit $290 when expectations for fiscal 2029 are around 28% over fiscal 2028's. Next to this fiscal year's expected 90% revenue growth and next year's forecast 70%, that would arguably be a low bar. But nothing about fiscal 2029 is guided yet. And estimates that far out can change a lot.\\nOf course, the valuation multiple is also the half Nvidia doesn't control. Shares have risen roughly 21% in 2026 as of this writing, a year when quarterly profit more than doubled. That mismatch is the price-to-earnings multiple shrinking, quarter by quarter.\\nIf the shrinking continues at anything like this year's pace, no forecast growth rate could take the stock to $7 trillion on this timeline.\\nIn the end, what separates this milestone from past ones is that the growth to support it is already forecast. Reaching $7 trillion doesn't need a reacceleration or a richer valuation than Broadcom gets today.\\nI think the earnings will show up more or less as forecast. What the market chooses to pay for them is another question.\\nBy Daniel Sparks – Sep 25, 2026 at 7:24PM EST\\nNvidia's market value is roughly $5.45 trillion as of this writing, leaving a gap of roughly $1.55 trillion to $7 trillion.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"figure\": 1,\n     \"text\": \"the fiscal second quarter's 75% gross margin\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 2,\n     \"text\": \"gross margin of 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n     \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n     \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?\",\n     \"period\": \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 9,\n   \"title\": \"China May Let Alibaba Buy New Nvidia Chips, The Information Says\",\n   \"publisher\": \"bloomberg.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-27\",\n   \"text\": \"(Bloomberg) -- The Chinese government has signaled it may allow companies such as Alibaba Group Holding Ltd. and ByteDance Ltd to purchase Nvidia Corp.'s new RTX Pro 5500 chips, The Information reported, citing people familiar with the matter.\\nEllison Pledges $9.2 Billion More in Oracle Shares for Loans\\nMcDonald's Selloff Hits 30% as Big Mac Inflation Spurs Pushback\\nThe Ministry of Industry and Information Technology recently asked some companies to report on their plans to purchase the high-end graphics processor chip, which was released this month, including how many of them and what they would use the product for, the report said, adding that the ministry told some of the firms it intends to approve the purchases.\\nNvidia has faced export restrictions from the US as well as curbs from Beijing over the sale of its chips to China, due to a web of bilateral tensions ranging from security to competition on artificial intelligence.\\nThe US administration recently lifted restrictions on some Nvidia chips, though the products tend to be older-generation chips that have been sold in small quantities.\\nGLP-1 Users Are Furious After Employers Drop Insurance Benefits\\nThis $2 Billion Company Dresses HBO's The Pitt Cast and Real Doctors Too\",\n   \"named_figures\": [],\n   \"open_questions\": null\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 10,\n   \"title\": \"Why Nvidia (NVDA) Stock Is Trading Up Today\",\n   \"publisher\": \"finance.yahoo.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-28\",\n   \"text\": \"Shares of leading designer of graphics chips Nvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA) jumped 2.7% in the morning session after the company authorized an additional $150 billion for its share buyback program, taking the total authorization to $235 billion.\\nAccording to CNBC, the chipmaker said the increase is the largest share-repurchase authorization boost in history and that it expects to complete the remaining buyback program through fiscal 2028. CEO Jensen Huang said in a statement that Nvidia's growth is being driven by a \\\"once-in-a-generation platform shift to AI and accelerated computing,\\\" and that strong cash generation lets the company invest in that buildout while returning capital to shareholders. Speaking on CNBC's \\\"Squawk Box,\\\" Huang called the AI infrastructure cycle the largest buildout in human history and said Nvidia plans to return more cash to shareholders as it generates it. A record buyback authorization typically supports the stock by signaling management confidence and reducing share count when cash flow is strong.\\nAfter the initial pop, the shares cooled down to $231.14, up 2.7% from the previous close.\\nNvidia's shares are not very volatile and have only had 8 moves greater than 5% over the last year. In that context, today's move indicates the market considers this news meaningful, although it might not be something that would fundamentally change its perception of the business.\\nThe biggest move we wrote about over the last year was about 1 month ago when the stock gained 8.3% on the news that the company reported second-quarter financial results that topped Wall Street expectations and projected 70% revenue growth for the next year (fiscal 2028).\\nAccording to a company press release and earnings commentary, Nvidia reported revenue of $96.22 billion for the second quarter, up 106% year-over-year, alongside adjusted earnings per share of $2.22. Both figures surpassed Wall Street consensus estimates, which called for revenue of $92.37 billion and adjusted earnings of $2.09 per share.\\nNvidia is up 22.4% since the beginning of the year, and at $231.14 per share, it is trading close to its 52-week high of $235.74 from May 2026. Investors who bought $1,000 worth of Nvidia's shares 5 years ago would now be looking at an investment worth $11,167.\\nALSO WORTH WATCHING: Nvidia's Quiet Partner. Nvidia's chips cost a hundred grand. The connectors that make them work cost even more. One company makes them all.\\nEvery AI server needs specialized infrastructure the chip companies don't make. High-speed cables. Power connectors. Thermal sensors. This 90-year-old company built a monopoly on it. The AI boom just started. This stock is still flying under the radar. Claim The Stock Ticker Here for FREE .\",\n   \"named_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"figure\": 1,\n     \"text\": \"the $92.37 billion revenue estimate for the quarter\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 2,\n     \"text\": \"the $96.22 billion revenue of the quarter\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n     \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n     \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?\",\n     \"period\": \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n     \"question\": \"Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology formally approve purchases of Nvidia's new RTX Pro 5500 high-end GPU by Alibaba, ByteDance and other Chinese firms by the end of Nvidia's fiscal 2027, allowing those sales to proceed?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3, because the item gives no date of its own.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  }\n ],\n \"answers\": [\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B21\",\n   \"article\": 7,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported data center revenue of $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027 out of $96 billion in total revenue, and management guided fiscal Q3 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, a midpoint the Trefis article calculates as about 12% above the Q2 total.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA’s data center revenue was $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total. Management guided fiscal Q3 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. That midpoint is about 12% above the Q2 total.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"data center segment\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"total revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"plus or minus 2%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA said memory prices rose more than it expected and it reset its margin expectations, guiding gross margin to 74% for fiscal Q3 2027, down from 75% in Q2, and expecting gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations.\",\n      \"NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3\",\n       \"value\": \"74%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q4\",\n       \"value\": \"71% to 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"expected bottom\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs, and management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA's business in fiscal 2028 — a portion of its business resting on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA has also invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs. Management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA’s balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA’s business in fiscal 2028. That part of NVIDIA’s business would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"investment in AI labs\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"nearly $50 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"share of NVIDIA's business from AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly a quarter\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Gives the scale of NVIDIA's equity investment in AI labs (nearly $50 billion) alongside management's expectation that balance-sheet-backed labs reach roughly a quarter of its fiscal 2028 business, informing the thesis on how much credit support and revenue backstop NVIDIA discloses versus equity investments.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n     \"summary\": \"Trefis calculated that NVIDIA's revenue grew 83% over the past year, against 16.7% for Texas Instruments, and that NVIDIA kept 65.2 cents of each sales dollar as operating profit, against 38.1 cents at TI.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA’s revenue grew 83% over the past year, against 16.7% for TI. NVIDIA kept 65.2 cents of each sales dollar as operating profit, against 38.1 cents at TI.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"trefis.com\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-09-25\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"over the past year\",\n       \"value\": \"83%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"trefis.com\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"operating profit as a share of sales\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"65.2 cents\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"of each sales dollar\",\n       \"party\": \"trefis.com\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B22\",\n   \"article\": 3,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported data center segment revenue of $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially, and consolidated revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 26 press release.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The Data Center segment remains NVIDIA's primary growth engine, with revenues jumping 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the Aug. 26 press release. Consolidated revenues soared to $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center segment revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Data Center segment; up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"consolidated revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"consolidated; up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% in the fiscal second quarter of 2027 from 72.5% a year earlier.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin increased to 75% in the fiscal second quarter compared with 72.5% a year ago.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"non-GAAP gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP; versus 72.5% a year earlier\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, which at the midpoint would represent a 12% sequential increase.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA further expects profitability to remain strong and projects revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027. At the midpoint, this would represent a 12% sequential rise.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"plus or minus 2%; midpoint implies a 12% sequential rise\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B23\",\n   \"article\": 2,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA told shareholders in May 2026 that it would raise its quarterly dividend to $0.25 per share from $0.01 per share — a 2,400% increase on Morningstar's calculation — which the article estimates returns roughly $25 billion a year to shareholders, enough to make NVIDIA one of the largest dividend payers in the US market.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"In May, Nvidia told shareholders that it would increase its quarterly payout to $0.25 per share from $0.01 per share. That’s a 2,400% increase, according to my calculations!\",\n      \"But in absolute terms, Nvidia’s dividend is huge. Something like $25 billion per year will be returned to shareholders. That sum will make Nvidia one of the largest dividend payers in the US stock market.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"May 2026\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"quarterly dividend per share\",\n       \"period\": \"quarterly, from the May 2026 declaration\",\n       \"value\": \"$0.25 per share\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"increased from $0.01 per share; 2,400% increase per the author's calculation\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"annual dividend payout to shareholders\",\n       \"period\": \"annual\",\n       \"value\": \"$25 billion per year\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"author's calculation ('something like'), derived from the raised quarterly payout\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA announced an $80 billion share buyback program at the time of its May 2026 dividend declaration; Morningstar notes most of NVIDIA's distributions to shareholders come as share repurchases rather than dividends.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Indeed, Nvidia announced an $80 billion share buyback program at the time of its dividend declaration.\",\n      \"“Most of Nvidia’s distributions to shareholders come in the form of share repurchases,” writes Colello.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"May 2026\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"share buyback program\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$80 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"announced at the time of the May 2026 dividend declaration\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has forecast 70% year-on-year revenue growth, cited by Morningstar as one of the major NVIDIA news items of 2026 and as inconsistent with the view that the dividend hike signals a slower-growth phase; the item does not state which period the 70% growth forecast covers.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Lost in a torrent of news about Nvidia NVDA this year—its role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments , its forecasts of 70% year-on-year revenue growth , and its acquisition of Hugging Face —was a dividend announcement.\",\n      \"Some might see Nvidia’s dividend hike as a sign the company is entering a new, more slow-growth life stage. That doesn’t fit with the 70% revenue growth forecast.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"year-on-year revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"NVIDIA forecast; period not specified in the item\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B24\",\n   \"article\": 6,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported Q2 fiscal 2027 revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 that beat consensus by 6.29%, and Data Center revenue of $89.023 billion; management guided Q3 fiscal 2027 revenue to $108 billion.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Q2 fiscal 2027 delivered revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%. Data Center revenue hit $89.023 billion. Management guided Q3 to $108 billion.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.22 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company total revenue, up 105.85% year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"non-GAAP EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.22\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP; beat consensus by 6.29%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89.023 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Data Center segment\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company guidance (excludes China Data Center compute revenue per a separate statement)\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n     \"summary\": \"The 24/7 Wall St. analyst lists as real risks to the NVIDIA thesis supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and days sales outstanding extending to 60 days.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"supply commitments\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$279 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"as cited by the analyst in the bear-case risk list; disclosure date and scope not stated\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"guarantee obligations (capped)\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$108.5 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"capped, as cited by the analyst in the bear-case risk list; counterparties not stated\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"days sales outstanding (DSO)\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"60 days\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"DSO extending to, per analyst\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"The cited $108.5 billion of capped guarantee obligations is the kind of credit support clean-1 tracks, indicating the current disclosed scale ahead of the next annual report; the item does not break out which partners or how much is new.\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-2\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"The cited $279 billion of supply commitments is a current disclosed scale for the purchase/supply commitment question; the item does not identify the reporting date of the balance or the counterparties.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, then recover to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Q4 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"71% to 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"expected to bottom on memory cost inflation; company margin outlook\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"expected recovery as volumes scale\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B25\",\n   \"article\": 10,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA authorized an additional $150 billion for its share buyback program, taking the total authorization to $235 billion. According to CNBC, the chipmaker said the increase is the largest share-repurchase authorization boost in history and that it expects to complete the remaining buyback program through fiscal 2028.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Shares of leading designer of graphics chips Nvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA) jumped 2.7% in the morning session after the company authorized an additional $150 billion for its share buyback program, taking the total authorization to $235 billion.\",\n      \"According to CNBC, the chipmaker said the increase is the largest share-repurchase authorization boost in history and that it expects to complete the remaining buyback program through fiscal 2028.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-09-28\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"additional share repurchase authorization\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$150 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"share buyback program\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"total share repurchase authorization\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$235 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"share buyback program, after the additional authorization\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported second-quarter revenue of $96.22 billion, up 106% year-over-year, alongside adjusted earnings per share of $2.22, according to a company press release and earnings commentary cited by the article. Both figures surpassed Wall Street consensus estimates of $92.37 billion in revenue and $2.09 in adjusted earnings per share.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"According to a company press release and earnings commentary, Nvidia reported revenue of $96.22 billion for the second quarter, up 106% year-over-year, alongside adjusted earnings per share of $2.22. Both figures surpassed Wall Street consensus estimates, which called for revenue of $92.37 billion and adjusted earnings of $2.09 per share.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"about 1 month ago\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.22 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth year-over-year\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"106%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"year-over-year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"adjusted earnings per share\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.22\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"adjusted\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$92.37 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Wall Street consensus estimate\",\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 5,\n       \"measure\": \"adjusted earnings per share\",\n       \"period\": \"second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.09\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"adjusted; Wall Street consensus estimate\",\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"Alongside its second-quarter results, reported about one month before the 28 September 2026 article, NVIDIA projected 70% revenue growth for the next year, fiscal 2028.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The biggest move we wrote about over the last year was about 1 month ago when the stock gained 8.3% on the news that the company reported second-quarter financial results that topped Wall Street expectations and projected 70% revenue growth for the next year (fiscal 2028).\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"about 1 month ago\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"next year (fiscal 2028)\",\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B26\",\n   \"article\": 5,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n     \"summary\": \"The Motley Fool column states, without naming a source for the figure, that the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data centers in 'this year' — the calendar year of the column's publication, 2026.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Considering the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data centers this year, that's huge growth still to come.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"fool.com (The Motley Fool)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"data center spending by the big five AI hyperscalers\",\n       \"period\": \"this year\",\n       \"value\": \"nearly $800 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"combined capital expenditure of the big five AI hyperscalers\",\n       \"party\": null\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B27\",\n   \"article\": 4,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, 2026 for the quarter ended July 26: revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year from $46.7 billion and up 18% sequentially, exceeding its prior guidance of $91 billion plus or minus 2%; non-GAAP net income rose 118% to $54 billion from $24.8 billion; adjusted EPS rose 120% to $2.22; non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%; operating income rose 124% year-over-year to $63.7 billion; Data Center segment revenue was $89 billion, up 117% year-over-year and the majority of total revenue.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia delivered another blockbuster quarter, reporting its fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, for the quarter ended July 26. Revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year (YoY) from $46.7 billion, and increased 18% sequentially. The result also comfortably exceeded Nvidia's prior guidance of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\",\n      \"The company's profitability expanded even faster. On a non-GAAP basis, net income climbed 118% to $54 billion, versus $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, while adjusted EPS jumped 120% to $2.22, exceeding expectations. Non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%. Operating income surged 124% YoY to $63.7 billion.\",\n      \"Nvidia's Data Center segment remained the primary growth engine, generating $89 billion in revenue, up 117% YoY from the prior-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027 (quarter ended July 26, 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"record; up 106% year-over-year and 18% sequentially\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Prior revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$91 billion, plus or minus 2%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company's prior guidance for the quarter, which the result exceeded\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP net income\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$54 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP; up 118% from $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"Adjusted EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.22\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"adjusted (non-GAAP); up 120%; exceeded expectations\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 5,\n       \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP; up from 72.5%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 6,\n       \"measure\": \"Operating income\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$63.7 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"up 124% year-over-year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 7,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Data Center segment; up 117% year-over-year; majority of total company revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, and GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points, with the forecast explicitly assuming no Data Center compute revenue from China, per the outlook issued with its Aug. 26, 2026 results.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Furthermore, Nvidia issued an even stronger outlook. For Q3 fiscal 2027, the company expects revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. Importantly, the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China. Nvidia expects GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion, plus or minus 2%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"GAAP gross margin guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"GAAP\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP gross margin guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-3\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Management's Q3 fiscal 2027 forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China, evidence on the current state of NVIDIA's China data-center business that the thesis asks whether export approval and Chinese acceptance will restore.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e7\",\n     \"summary\": \"Analysts, as reported by barchart.com, forecast NVIDIA EPS growth of 102.4% year-over-year to $9.25 in fiscal 2027, followed by a further 65.7% increase to $15.33 in fiscal 2028.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Street expects Nvidia's momentum to continue, with analysts forecasting EPS growth of 102.4% YoY to $9.25 in fiscal 2027, followed by another 65.7% increase to $15.33 in fiscal 2028.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Analysts (Street consensus)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$9.25\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"analysts' forecast; growth of 102.4% year-over-year\",\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"$15.33\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"analysts' forecast; further growth of 65.7%\",\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B28\",\n   \"article\": 9,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": \"none kept\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B29\",\n   \"article\": 8,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 second-quarter revenue (quarter ended July 26, 2026) of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year — faster than the 85% year-over-year growth of its fiscal first quarter — with the data center business contributing $89 billion of the total and net income of $59.7 billion, up 126% year over year, as stated in the company's second-quarter earnings release.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter (period ended July 26, 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company-reported, fiscal 2027 second quarter ended July 26, 2026\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"106%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 first quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"85%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"Data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"data center segment, part of total revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 5,\n       \"measure\": \"Net income\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$59.7 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 6,\n       \"measure\": \"Net income growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"126%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's guidance for its fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue, around 12% above fiscal second-quarter revenue, as cited by fool.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Guidance for the fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue , around 12% above fiscal Q2.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal third quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Guidance growth versus fiscal Q2 revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal third quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"around 12%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"sequential, versus fiscal Q2\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"On NVIDIA's late-August 2026 earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said the company expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending January 2028, and described it as \\\"a supply-constrained outlook.\\\"\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \\\"a supply-constrained outlook.\\\"\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"late August 2026\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028 (year ending January 2028)\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"described by the CFO as \\\"a supply-constrained outlook\\\"\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-2\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"The CFO's statement that fiscal 2028 growth is limited by manufacturing capacity, not customer demand, bears on how much NVIDIA must commit to purchase and supply capacity for that growth; it does not itself quantify any purchase or supply commitment.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e4\",\n     \"summary\": \"On the late-August 2026 earnings call, NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes take effect, a decline from the fiscal second quarter's 75%.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"late August 2026\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin (bottom)\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal fourth quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"between 71% and 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin (settling level)\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"as price hikes kick in\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e5\",\n     \"summary\": \"Analysts' consensus has NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion, as reported by fool.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Analysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"consensus (analysts)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$412 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"analysts' consensus\",\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B30\",\n   \"article\": 7,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported data center revenue of $89 billion within $96 billion of total revenue for fiscal Q2 2027, and management guided fiscal Q3 2027 total revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2% — a midpoint about 12% above the Q2 total — as reported by Trefis from the company's latest earnings call.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA’s data center revenue was $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total. Management guided fiscal Q3 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. That midpoint is about 12% above the Q2 total.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"data center segment of NVIDIA's total revenue, as reported\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"total revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company total\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion, plus or minus 2%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company total revenue, guided range of plus or minus 2%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA said memory prices rose more than it expected and that it reset its margin expectations: it guided gross margin to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in fiscal Q2, and expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Each company pays for its position. Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations.\",\n      \"NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"74%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q4 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"71% to 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"management's expected bottom for gross margin\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e4\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs, and management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA's business in fiscal 2028 — revenue partly resting on customers the company helps fund.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA has also invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs. Management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA’s balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA’s business in fiscal 2028. That part of NVIDIA’s business would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"investment in AI labs\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"nearly $50 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"NVIDIA's investment in AI labs, cumulative, as stated\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"AI labs' share of NVIDIA's business\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly a quarter of NVIDIA’s business\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"business from AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet; management expectation\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"States the scale of NVIDIA's equity investment in AI labs (nearly $50 billion) and management's expectation that balance-sheet-backed AI labs reach roughly a quarter of its business in fiscal 2028 — directly the investment-versus-support comparison the thesis tracks at the next annual report.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  }\n ]\n}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields/reader-packet-1-part3.json\\\"));\nfor a in p[\\\"articles\\\"]:\n print(\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"],a[\\\"title\\\"],\\\"DATE\\\",a[\\\"publication_date\\\"],\\\"NAMED\\\",a.get(\\\"named_figures\\\"),\\\"QUESTIONS\\\",a.get(\\\"open_questions\\\")); print(\\\"TEXT\\\",a[\\\"text\\\"] if a[\\\"article\\\"] in [5,6,7,8,9] else \\\"\\\")\nfor a in p[\\\"answers\\\"]:\n print(\\\"ANSWER\\\",a[\\\"answer\\\"],\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"]);\n for s in a[\\\"statements_with_figures\\\"] if isinstance(a[\\\"statements_with_figures\\\"],list) else []:\n  print(\\\"ST\\\",s[\\\"id\\\"],s[\\\"statement_party\\\"],s[\\\"statement_date\\\"],\\\"SUMMARY\\\",s[\\\"summary\\\"],\\\"FIG\\\",[(f[\\\"n\\\"],f[\\\"measure\\\"],f[\\\"value\\\"],f[\\\"kind\\\"],f[\\\"party\\\"]) for f in s[\\\"figures\\\"]],\\\"LINK\\\",s.get(\\\"links\\\",\\\"absent\\\"))'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields/reader-packet-1-part3.json\\\"));\nfor a in p[\\\"articles\\\"]:\n print(\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"],a[\\\"title\\\"],\\\"DATE\\\",a[\\\"publication_date\\\"],\\\"NAMED\\\",a.get(\\\"named_figures\\\"),\\\"QUESTIONS\\\",a.get(\\\"open_questions\\\")); print(\\\"TEXT\\\",a[\\\"text\\\"] if a[\\\"article\\\"] in [5,6,7,8,9] else \\\"\\\")\nfor a in p[\\\"answers\\\"]:\n print(\\\"ANSWER\\\",a[\\\"answer\\\"],\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"]);\n for s in a[\\\"statements_with_figures\\\"] if isinstance(a[\\\"statements_with_figures\\\"],list) else []:\n  print(\\\"ST\\\",s[\\\"id\\\"],s[\\\"statement_party\\\"],s[\\\"statement_date\\\"],\\\"SUMMARY\\\",s[\\\"summary\\\"],\\\"FIG\\\",[(f[\\\"n\\\"],f[\\\"measure\\\"],f[\\\"value\\\"],f[\\\"kind\\\"],f[\\\"party\\\"]) for f in s[\\\"figures\\\"]],\\\"LINK\\\",s.get(\\\"links\\\",\\\"absent\\\"))'\"","aggregated_output":"ARTICLE 2 Nvidia’s Immense Dividend Hike Puts Dividend Growth Stocks in the Spotlight DATE 2026-09-23 NAMED [{'figure': 1, 'text': 'about $25 billion a year returned through the dividend'}, {'figure': 2, 'text': 'the 2,400% increase of the dividend'}, {'figure': 3, 'text': 'the $0.25 quarterly dividend'}] QUESTIONS [{'id': 'clean-1', 'question': 'What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?', 'period': \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {'id': 'clean-2', 'question': 'How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?', 'period': 'Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).'}]\nTEXT \nARTICLE 3 Sandisk vs. NVIDIA: Which AI Stock Is the Better Buy Now? DATE 2026-09-23 NAMED [] QUESTIONS None\nTEXT \nARTICLE 4 Dear Nvidia Stock Fans, Mark Your Calendars for September 24 DATE 2026-09-23 NAMED [] QUESTIONS [{'id': 'clean-1', 'question': 'What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?', 'period': \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {'id': 'clean-2', 'question': 'How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?', 'period': 'Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).'}, {'id': 'clean-3', 'question': 'Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?', 'period': 'NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).'}, {'id': 'clean-4', 'question': \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\", 'period': \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"}]\nTEXT \nARTICLE 5 I've Held Nvidia for Nearly 3 Years. Here's Whether I'd Still Buy Today. DATE 2026-09-23 NAMED [] QUESTIONS None\nTEXT Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA) has been one of the best stocks to own in the artificial intelligence (AI) arms race. It is up nearly 1,500% since 2023 began, providing investors with incredible returns. Unfortunately, I do not have those returns in my portfolio.\nI bought the stock at the beginning of 2023 and watched it double. Then I sold the stock because I was worried that AI computing demand wouldn't last. I was very wrong. I had to disregard all of the prior gains and purchase shares in 2024 after it had run up some more, which has still been a great investment for me.\nBut would I buy the stock again today? You bet I would.\nNvidia is still a dominant force in the industry\nNvidia is turning the investment world on its head. Normally, the law of large numbers dictates that the larger a company is, the harder it is to grow. Nvidia is the world's largest company and has reached heights that no other company has ever achieved. However, it's growing faster than any other stock among the world's top 10 companies. That's hard to believe, but it showcases just how massive the demand is for AI computing power.\nNvidia makes GPUs , which are the go-to computing unit for all sorts of AI operations, be it training or inference. While there are alternatives, the Nvidia ecosystem cannot be beaten. Additionally, custom AI chips are starting to become more popular among many AI hyperscalers. Still, there will always be a place for computing units like Nvidia's GPUs that are flexible and can handle a wide variety of workloads.\nDue to Nvidia's market position, it has advanced knowledge of how much computing power the AI hyperscalers are planning. The AI companies are making multiyear plans to ensure they have mapped out when they will need computing units, because data centers don't go up overnight. This gives Nvidia the ability to issue long-term guidance, and the guidance regarding the AI build-out is simply incredible.\nNvidia estimates that global data center capital expenditures will rise to between $3 trillion and $4 trillion by 2030. Considering the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data centers this year, that's huge growth still to come.\nSo, if you think you've missed the boat on Nvidia, think again. There is still plenty of upside to come, and Nvidia will be one of the biggest beneficiaries of the AI build-out. However, none of it is priced into Nvidia's stock.\nThat's pretty much in line with where other big tech stocks trade when one-time effects don't skew their valuations, but there's one problem: Nvidia is growing far faster than any of its big tech peers. The trailing price-to-earnings ratio doesn't account for Nvidia's massive growth ahead, and that's where a forward-looking metric helps out.\nAt less than 15 times next year's earnings, Nvidia's stock has almost no growth priced into it today. That means the stock has major upside over the next year. Furthermore, with the AI build-out expected to intensify through 2030, that leaves plenty more room for Nvidia to run.\nEven if you've missed out on Nvidia so far, now is the perfect time to buy more. Very little growth has been priced in, and Nvidia's stock looks well positioned to be one of the best investments over the next few years.\nARTICLE 6 Nvidia Stock Set For 40% Surge: 3 Catalysts That Could Drive the Next Big Move DATE 2026-09-23 NAMED [{'figure': 1, 'text': 'fiscal 2028 growth of approximately 70%'}, {'figure': 2, 'text': 'NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand'}] QUESTIONS [{'id': 'clean-1', 'question': 'What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?', 'period': \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {'id': 'clean-2', 'question': 'How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?', 'period': 'Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).'}, {'id': 'clean-3', 'question': 'Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?', 'period': 'NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).'}, {'id': 'clean-4', 'question': \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\", 'period': \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"}, {'id': 'clean-5', 'question': 'How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?', 'period': \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"}]\nTEXT NVDA earns a BUY with a $310.20 target (~40% upside), driven by the Vera Rubin ramp and $500 billion in mobilized AI infrastructure capital.\nAMD's trailing P/E of 211 dwarfs NVDA's 45, while AVGO's AI semiconductor revenue surged 221%, confirming the AI infrastructure wave underpinning NVDA's outlook.\nMy 24/7 Wall St. price target for NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA ) is $310.20, which implies 39.56% upside from the current $225.35 quote. I rate NVDA a buy with high conviction.\nThree catalysts drive the call: the Vera Rubin production ramp, more than $500 billion in third-party AI infrastructure capital being mobilized through NVIDIA partners, and the opening chapter of physical AI across robotics and autonomous vehicles.\nNVDA is up 21.11% year to date and 28.17% over the trailing year. Q2 fiscal 2027 delivered revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%. Data Center revenue hit $89.023 billion. Management guided Q3 to $108 billion.\nWhy Bulls See $353 or Higher\nThe bull-case one-year target is $353.54. Jensen Huang told investors NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand, with fiscal 2028 growth pegged at approximately 70%. Vera Rubin ramps platform value per gigawatt from Blackwell's $25 billion to $40 billion.\nHyperscaler capex is expected to reach nearly 800 billion in 2026 and 1.3 trillion in 2027, a wave that also pulls in the power, cooling, and networking suppliers we profiled in a free report on seven AI infrastructure names that aren't chipmakers. Analyst sentiment shows 57 Buy or Strong Buy ratings against 2 Holds and 1 Sell.\nThe bear scenario bottoms at $261.39, still above current price. Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\nQ3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue citing geopolitical uncertainty. Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\nAdvanced Micro Devices ( NASDAQ:AMD ) posted Q2 fiscal 2026 revenue of $11.536 billion, up 50.11%, with Data Center up 107%. AMD trades at a trailing P/E of 211 versus NVDA's 45, making NVDA the cheaper name on growth per valuation dollar.\nBroadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ) competes in custom AI ASICs and networking. Q3 fiscal 2026 revenue reached $29.591 billion, up 85.5%, with AI semiconductor revenue of $16.70 billion growing 221% year over year. Its acceleration validates NVDA's guided $108.0 billion quarter sits inside a real industry wave. The peer set makes $310.20 look reasonable.\nI'd own NVDA at $225.35 with a 24/7 Wall St. price target of $310.20 and a buy rating at 90% confidence. Supply-constrained demand into a Vera Rubin ramp management calls the fastest in company history drives the call.\nI'd stay a buyer if hyperscaler capex tracks toward 1.3 trillion in 2027. I'd step aside if memory pricing pushes gross margin below 71% or if China restrictions widen beyond the Data Center compute carve-out.\nThese projections assume NVIDIA executes on the Vera Rubin ramp and hyperscaler AI spending holds. Significant upside or downside could result from broader China market access, a major AI capex reset, or accelerated adoption of physical AI and robotics platforms.\nARTICLE 7 What Does NVIDIA Offer That Texas Instruments Does Not? DATE 2026-09-25 NAMED [] QUESTIONS [{'id': 'clean-1', 'question': 'What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?', 'period': \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {'id': 'clean-2', 'question': 'How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?', 'period': 'Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).'}, {'id': 'clean-3', 'question': 'Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?', 'period': 'NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).'}, {'id': 'clean-4', 'question': \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\", 'period': \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"}, {'id': 'clean-5', 'question': 'How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?', 'period': \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"}]\nTEXT What Does NVIDIA Offer That Texas Instruments Does Not?\nTexas Instruments (TXN) and NVIDIA both sell chips for the data centers now being built. Data centers bring in nearly all of NVIDIA’s revenue, while TI sells to industrial and automotive customers as well as data centers. You pay 38.2 times the yearly profit for TI, yet only 28.1 times for NVIDIA. Their latest earnings calls show the two businesses parting ways on supply.\nNVIDIA’s data center revenue was $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total. Management guided fiscal Q3 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. That midpoint is about 12% above the Q2 total. Management expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028.\nTI guided Q3 2026 revenue to $5.65 billion to $6.15 billion, against $5.5 billion in Q2. TI’s data center sales doubled from a year earlier in Q2. Management said most of the Q3 growth will come from selling more units, not from higher prices. TI also has clean room space it can equip if demand keeps rising.\nThe two outlooks are different kinds of news. Demand for NVIDIA’s chips is larger than NVIDIA’s supply, so supply sets the pace. TI has spare room to make more, so demand sets TI’s pace.\nEach company pays for its position. Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations. Gross margin is the share of sales left after production costs. NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\nNVIDIA has also invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs. Management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA’s balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA’s business in fiscal 2028. That part of NVIDIA’s business would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\nTI’s cost is its factories. TI spent $3.3 billion on capital projects in the year to Q2 2026. TI’s $8.7 billion of cash from operations covered that more than twice over. TI’s free cash flow over the same period included $1.6 billion of CHIPS Act incentives. The yearly numbers on growth, margin, and debt show how far apart the two already are.\nThis comparison scores six standard measures of price, growth, profit and debt , and NVIDIA leads Texas Instruments on all six. NVIDIA’s revenue grew 83% over the past year, against 16.7% for TI. NVIDIA kept 65.2 cents of each sales dollar as operating profit, against 38.1 cents at TI.\nDebt equals 0.7% of NVIDIA’s market value, against 6.1% for TI. NVIDIA carries almost no debt against its value.\nTI wins on one measure outside those six: price to sales. You pay 11.9 times the yearly revenue for TI, against 17.9 times for NVIDIA. The six scored measures judge price on profit, and on profit NVIDIA is cheaper.\nTI also has a long dividend record. In September, TI raised the quarterly dividend to $1.52 a share, from $1.42. That was TI’s 23rd straight year of dividend increases.\nWhat Would Count As A Stumble For NVIDIA?\nNVIDIA’s lead is wide enough that it would take a real stumble to shrink it. Revenue below NVIDIA’s own guided range for fiscal Q3 would be the first sign of one. NVIDIA holds its call on that quarter on November 17. A result inside or above the range would leave NVIDIA’s lead in place.\nNVIDIA leads on growth, margins, debt, and price on profit. TI’s strengths are a lower price on sales, spare capacity, and a long dividend record. Revenue below NVIDIA’s guided range in November is the first result that would test that lead.\nHow To Act On TXN?\nARTICLE 8 Nvidia Would Have to Add Almost a Whole Broadcom to Reach $7 Trillion DATE 2026-09-25 NAMED [{'figure': 1, 'text': \"the fiscal second quarter's 75% gross margin\"}, {'figure': 2, 'text': 'gross margin of 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028'}] QUESTIONS [{'id': 'clean-1', 'question': 'What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?', 'period': \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {'id': 'clean-2', 'question': 'How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?', 'period': 'Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).'}, {'id': 'clean-3', 'question': 'Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?', 'period': 'NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).'}, {'id': 'clean-4', 'question': \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\", 'period': \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"}, {'id': 'clean-5', 'question': 'How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?', 'period': \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"}, {'id': 'clean-6', 'question': 'Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?', 'period': \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"}]\nTEXT Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) , the world's most valuable company, is worth around $5.45 trillion as of this writing. The next big round number is $7 trillion. And the gap between them is larger than it sounds: roughly $1.55 trillion, or nearly the full market value of Broadcom ( AVGO +2.83% ) , the $1.69 trillion chip and infrastructure software business.\nWhat would have to be true of Nvidia's revenue, its margins, and its price-to-earnings multiple for the company to add almost a whole Broadcom to its value? And how long could it take?\nAfter all, fewer than 10 other companies in the world are worth more than the gap itself. At Nvidia's size, even a modest-sounding percentage gain today matches an entire giant company.\nStep back, though, and the climb looks smaller than the dollars suggest. From around $225 as I write this, a $7 trillion market cap comes out to around $290 a share -- a rise of around 28%.\nFor context, shares have climbed 37% from their 52-week low, and they now trade within around 5% of their 52-week high. I'd say nothing about a 28% move is unusual for this stock.\nNvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\n\"Now, compute is revenue,\" said CEO Jensen Huang in the company's second-quarter earnings release.\nThe forecasts go far past the quarter. Guidance for the fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue , around 12% above fiscal Q2. And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \"a supply-constrained outlook.\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.\nAnalysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion. Grow that by roughly 70%, and fiscal 2028 lands somewhere around $700 billion.\nMargins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\nWhen might it happen?\nNvidia's stock costs around 14 times forward earnings today, using analysts' fiscal 2028 estimates. At $290 per share, that forward price-to-earnings multiple would sit closer to 18 -- around what Broadcom gets today on the same basis.\nFiscal 2028 ends in January 2028, roughly 16 months from now. If Nvidia delivers the year it has forecast, and the market prices those earnings the way it prices Broadcom's today, $7 trillion arrives somewhere on that timeline.\nThe stock could also get there without any increase in its price-to-earnings multiple. Hold that multiple of expected earnings flat, and shares hit $290 when expectations for fiscal 2029 are around 28% over fiscal 2028's. Next to this fiscal year's expected 90% revenue growth and next year's forecast 70%, that would arguably be a low bar. But nothing about fiscal 2029 is guided yet. And estimates that far out can change a lot.\nOf course, the valuation multiple is also the half Nvidia doesn't control. Shares have risen roughly 21% in 2026 as of this writing, a year when quarterly profit more than doubled. That mismatch is the price-to-earnings multiple shrinking, quarter by quarter.\nIf the shrinking continues at anything like this year's pace, no forecast growth rate could take the stock to $7 trillion on this timeline.\nIn the end, what separates this milestone from past ones is that the growth to support it is already forecast. Reaching $7 trillion doesn't need a reacceleration or a richer valuation than Broadcom gets today.\nI think the earnings will show up more or less as forecast. What the market chooses to pay for them is another question.\nBy Daniel Sparks – Sep 25, 2026 at 7:24PM EST\nNvidia's market value is roughly $5.45 trillion as of this writing, leaving a gap of roughly $1.55 trillion to $7 trillion.\nARTICLE 9 China May Let Alibaba Buy New Nvidia Chips, The Information Says DATE 2026-09-27 NAMED [] QUESTIONS None\nTEXT (Bloomberg) -- The Chinese government has signaled it may allow companies such as Alibaba Group Holding Ltd. and ByteDance Ltd to purchase Nvidia Corp.'s new RTX Pro 5500 chips, The Information reported, citing people familiar with the matter.\nEllison Pledges $9.2 Billion More in Oracle Shares for Loans\nMcDonald's Selloff Hits 30% as Big Mac Inflation Spurs Pushback\nThe Ministry of Industry and Information Technology recently asked some companies to report on their plans to purchase the high-end graphics processor chip, which was released this month, including how many of them and what they would use the product for, the report said, adding that the ministry told some of the firms it intends to approve the purchases.\nNvidia has faced export restrictions from the US as well as curbs from Beijing over the sale of its chips to China, due to a web of bilateral tensions ranging from security to competition on artificial intelligence.\nThe US administration recently lifted restrictions on some Nvidia chips, though the products tend to be older-generation chips that have been sold in small quantities.\nGLP-1 Users Are Furious After Employers Drop Insurance Benefits\nThis $2 Billion Company Dresses HBO's The Pitt Cast and Real Doctors Too\nARTICLE 10 Why Nvidia (NVDA) Stock Is Trading Up Today DATE 2026-09-28 NAMED [{'figure': 1, 'text': 'the $92.37 billion revenue estimate for the quarter'}, {'figure': 2, 'text': 'the $96.22 billion revenue of the quarter'}] QUESTIONS [{'id': 'clean-1', 'question': 'What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?', 'period': \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {'id': 'clean-2', 'question': 'How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?', 'period': 'Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).'}, {'id': 'clean-3', 'question': 'Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?', 'period': 'NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).'}, {'id': 'clean-4', 'question': \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\", 'period': \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"}, {'id': 'clean-5', 'question': 'How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?', 'period': \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"}, {'id': 'clean-6', 'question': 'Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?', 'period': \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"}, {'id': 'clean-7', 'question': \"Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology formally approve purchases of Nvidia's new RTX Pro 5500 high-end GPU by Alibaba, ByteDance and other Chinese firms by the end of Nvidia's fiscal 2027, allowing those sales to proceed?\", 'period': 'NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3, because the item gives no date of its own.'}]\nTEXT \nANSWER B21 ARTICLE 7\nST ev-1 NVIDIA None SUMMARY NVIDIA reported data center revenue of $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027 out of $96 billion in total revenue, and management guided fiscal Q3 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, a midpoint the Trefis article calculates as about 12% above the Q2 total. FIG [(1, 'data center revenue', '$89 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (2, 'total revenue', '$96 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (3, 'revenue guidance', '$108 billion', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA management')] LINK none\nST ev-3 NVIDIA None SUMMARY NVIDIA said memory prices rose more than it expected and it reset its margin expectations, guiding gross margin to 74% for fiscal Q3 2027, down from 75% in Q2, and expecting gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4. FIG [(1, 'gross margin', '74%', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA'), (2, 'gross margin', '75%', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (3, 'gross margin', '71% to 72%', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA')] LINK none\nST ev-4 NVIDIA None SUMMARY NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs, and management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA's business in fiscal 2028 — a portion of its business resting on customers NVIDIA helps fund. FIG [(1, 'investment in AI labs', 'nearly $50 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (2, \"share of NVIDIA's business from AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet\", 'roughly a quarter', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA management')] LINK [{'question': 'clean-1', 'new_question': False, 'reason': \"Gives the scale of NVIDIA's equity investment in AI labs (nearly $50 billion) alongside management's expectation that balance-sheet-backed labs reach roughly a quarter of its fiscal 2028 business, informing the thesis on how much credit support and revenue backstop NVIDIA discloses versus equity investments.\"}]\nST ev-5 trefis.com 2026-09-25 SUMMARY Trefis calculated that NVIDIA's revenue grew 83% over the past year, against 16.7% for Texas Instruments, and that NVIDIA kept 65.2 cents of each sales dollar as operating profit, against 38.1 cents at TI. FIG [(1, 'revenue growth', '83%', 'actual', 'trefis.com'), (2, 'operating profit as a share of sales', '65.2 cents', 'actual', 'trefis.com')] LINK none\nANSWER B22 ARTICLE 3\nST e1 NVIDIA 2026-08-26 SUMMARY NVIDIA reported data center segment revenue of $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially, and consolidated revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 26 press release. FIG [(1, 'Data Center segment revenue', '$89 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (2, 'consolidated revenue', '$96.2 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA')] LINK absent\nST e2 NVIDIA 2026-08-26 SUMMARY NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% in the fiscal second quarter of 2027 from 72.5% a year earlier. FIG [(1, 'non-GAAP gross margin', '75%', 'actual', 'NVIDIA')] LINK absent\nST e3 NVIDIA 2026-08-26 SUMMARY NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, which at the midpoint would represent a 12% sequential increase. FIG [(1, 'revenue guidance', '$108 billion', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA')] LINK absent\nANSWER B23 ARTICLE 2\nST ev-1 NVIDIA May 2026 SUMMARY NVIDIA told shareholders in May 2026 that it would raise its quarterly dividend to $0.25 per share from $0.01 per share — a 2,400% increase on Morningstar's calculation — which the article estimates returns roughly $25 billion a year to shareholders, enough to make NVIDIA one of the largest dividend payers in the US market. FIG [(1, 'quarterly dividend per share', '$0.25 per share', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (2, 'annual dividend payout to shareholders', '$25 billion per year', 'estimate', 'NVIDIA')] LINK none\nST ev-2 NVIDIA May 2026 SUMMARY NVIDIA announced an $80 billion share buyback program at the time of its May 2026 dividend declaration; Morningstar notes most of NVIDIA's distributions to shareholders come as share repurchases rather than dividends. FIG [(1, 'share buyback program', '$80 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA')] LINK none\nST ev-3 NVIDIA None SUMMARY NVIDIA has forecast 70% year-on-year revenue growth, cited by Morningstar as one of the major NVIDIA news items of 2026 and as inconsistent with the view that the dividend hike signals a slower-growth phase; the item does not state which period the 70% growth forecast covers. FIG [(1, 'year-on-year revenue growth', '70%', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA')] LINK none\nANSWER B24 ARTICLE 6\nST ev-1 NVIDIA None SUMMARY NVIDIA reported Q2 fiscal 2027 revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 that beat consensus by 6.29%, and Data Center revenue of $89.023 billion; management guided Q3 fiscal 2027 revenue to $108 billion. FIG [(1, 'revenue', '$96.22 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (2, 'non-GAAP EPS', '$2.22', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (3, 'Data Center revenue', '$89.023 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (4, 'revenue guidance', '$108 billion', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA')] LINK none\nST ev-3 NVIDIA None SUMMARY The 24/7 Wall St. analyst lists as real risks to the NVIDIA thesis supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and days sales outstanding extending to 60 days. FIG [(1, 'supply commitments', '$279 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (2, 'guarantee obligations (capped)', '$108.5 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (3, 'days sales outstanding (DSO)', '60 days', 'actual', 'NVIDIA')] LINK [{'question': 'clean-1', 'new_question': False, 'reason': 'The cited $108.5 billion of capped guarantee obligations is the kind of credit support clean-1 tracks, indicating the current disclosed scale ahead of the next annual report; the item does not break out which partners or how much is new.'}, {'question': 'clean-2', 'new_question': False, 'reason': 'The cited $279 billion of supply commitments is a current disclosed scale for the purchase/supply commitment question; the item does not identify the reporting date of the balance or the counterparties.'}]\nST ev-5 NVIDIA None SUMMARY NVIDIA's gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, then recover to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale. FIG [(1, 'gross margin', '71% to 72%', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA'), (2, 'gross margin', '72% to 73%', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA')] LINK none\nANSWER B25 ARTICLE 10\nST e1 NVIDIA 2026-09-28 SUMMARY NVIDIA authorized an additional $150 billion for its share buyback program, taking the total authorization to $235 billion. According to CNBC, the chipmaker said the increase is the largest share-repurchase authorization boost in history and that it expects to complete the remaining buyback program through fiscal 2028. FIG [(1, 'additional share repurchase authorization', '$150 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (2, 'total share repurchase authorization', '$235 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA')] LINK none\nST e2 NVIDIA about 1 month ago SUMMARY NVIDIA reported second-quarter revenue of $96.22 billion, up 106% year-over-year, alongside adjusted earnings per share of $2.22, according to a company press release and earnings commentary cited by the article. Both figures surpassed Wall Street consensus estimates of $92.37 billion in revenue and $2.09 in adjusted earnings per share. FIG [(1, 'revenue', '$96.22 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (2, 'revenue growth year-over-year', '106%', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (3, 'adjusted earnings per share', '$2.22', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (4, 'revenue', '$92.37 billion', 'estimate', 'consensus'), (5, 'adjusted earnings per share', '$2.09', 'estimate', 'consensus')] LINK none\nST e3 NVIDIA about 1 month ago SUMMARY Alongside its second-quarter results, reported about one month before the 28 September 2026 article, NVIDIA projected 70% revenue growth for the next year, fiscal 2028. FIG [(1, 'revenue growth', '70%', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA')] LINK none\nANSWER B26 ARTICLE 5\nST ev-2 fool.com (The Motley Fool) None SUMMARY The Motley Fool column states, without naming a source for the figure, that the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data centers in 'this year' — the calendar year of the column's publication, 2026. FIG [(1, 'data center spending by the big five AI hyperscalers', 'nearly $800 billion', 'estimate', None)] LINK absent\nANSWER B27 ARTICLE 4\nST e2 NVIDIA 2026-08-26 SUMMARY NVIDIA reported fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, 2026 for the quarter ended July 26: revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year from $46.7 billion and up 18% sequentially, exceeding its prior guidance of $91 billion plus or minus 2%; non-GAAP net income rose 118% to $54 billion from $24.8 billion; adjusted EPS rose 120% to $2.22; non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%; operating income rose 124% year-over-year to $63.7 billion; Data Center segment revenue was $89 billion, up 117% year-over-year and the majority of total revenue. FIG [(1, 'Revenue', '$96.2 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (2, 'Prior revenue guidance', '$91 billion, plus or minus 2%', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA'), (3, 'Non-GAAP net income', '$54 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (4, 'Adjusted EPS', '$2.22', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (5, 'Non-GAAP gross margin', '75%', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (6, 'Operating income', '$63.7 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (7, 'Data Center revenue', '$89 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA')] LINK none\nST e3 NVIDIA 2026-08-26 SUMMARY NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, and GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points, with the forecast explicitly assuming no Data Center compute revenue from China, per the outlook issued with its Aug. 26, 2026 results. FIG [(1, 'Revenue guidance', '$108 billion, plus or minus 2%', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA'), (2, 'GAAP gross margin guidance', 'approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA'), (3, 'Non-GAAP gross margin guidance', 'approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA')] LINK [{'question': 'clean-3', 'new_question': False, 'reason': \"Management's Q3 fiscal 2027 forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China, evidence on the current state of NVIDIA's China data-center business that the thesis asks whether export approval and Chinese acceptance will restore.\"}]\nST e7 Analysts (Street consensus) None SUMMARY Analysts, as reported by barchart.com, forecast NVIDIA EPS growth of 102.4% year-over-year to $9.25 in fiscal 2027, followed by a further 65.7% increase to $15.33 in fiscal 2028. FIG [(1, 'EPS', '$9.25', 'estimate', 'consensus'), (2, 'EPS', '$15.33', 'estimate', 'consensus')] LINK none\nANSWER B28 ARTICLE 9\nANSWER B29 ARTICLE 8\nST e1 NVIDIA None SUMMARY NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 second-quarter revenue (quarter ended July 26, 2026) of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year — faster than the 85% year-over-year growth of its fiscal first quarter — with the data center business contributing $89 billion of the total and net income of $59.7 billion, up 126% year over year, as stated in the company's second-quarter earnings release. FIG [(1, 'Revenue', '$96.2 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (2, 'Revenue growth year over year', '106%', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (3, 'Revenue growth year over year', '85%', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (4, 'Data center revenue', '$89 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (5, 'Net income', '$59.7 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (6, 'Net income growth year over year', '126%', 'actual', 'NVIDIA')] LINK none\nST e2 NVIDIA None SUMMARY NVIDIA's guidance for its fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue, around 12% above fiscal second-quarter revenue, as cited by fool.com. FIG [(1, 'Revenue guidance', '$108 billion', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA'), (2, 'Guidance growth versus fiscal Q2 revenue', 'around 12%', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA')] LINK none\nST e3 NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress) late August 2026 SUMMARY On NVIDIA's late-August 2026 earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said the company expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending January 2028, and described it as \"a supply-constrained outlook.\" FIG [(1, 'Revenue growth year over year', 'roughly 70%', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)')] LINK [{'question': 'clean-2', 'new_question': False, 'reason': \"The CFO's statement that fiscal 2028 growth is limited by manufacturing capacity, not customer demand, bears on how much NVIDIA must commit to purchase and supply capacity for that growth; it does not itself quantify any purchase or supply commitment.\"}]\nST e4 NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress) late August 2026 SUMMARY On the late-August 2026 earnings call, NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes take effect, a decline from the fiscal second quarter's 75%. FIG [(1, 'Gross margin (bottom)', 'between 71% and 72%', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)'), (2, 'Gross margin (settling level)', '72% to 73%', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)'), (3, 'Gross margin', '75%', 'actual', 'NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)')] LINK none\nST e5 consensus (analysts) None SUMMARY Analysts' consensus has NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion, as reported by fool.com. FIG [(1, 'Revenue', '$412 billion', 'estimate', 'consensus')] LINK none\nANSWER B30 ARTICLE 7\nST e1 NVIDIA None SUMMARY NVIDIA reported data center revenue of $89 billion within $96 billion of total revenue for fiscal Q2 2027, and management guided fiscal Q3 2027 total revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2% — a midpoint about 12% above the Q2 total — as reported by Trefis from the company's latest earnings call. FIG [(1, 'data center revenue', '$89 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (2, 'total revenue', '$96 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (3, 'revenue guidance', '$108 billion, plus or minus 2%', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA')] LINK none\nST e3 NVIDIA None SUMMARY NVIDIA said memory prices rose more than it expected and that it reset its margin expectations: it guided gross margin to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in fiscal Q2, and expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4. FIG [(1, 'gross margin', '75%', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (2, 'gross margin guidance', '74%', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA'), (3, 'gross margin', '71% to 72%', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA')] LINK none\nST e4 NVIDIA None SUMMARY NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs, and management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA's business in fiscal 2028 — revenue partly resting on customers the company helps fund. FIG [(1, 'investment in AI labs', 'nearly $50 billion', 'actual', 'NVIDIA'), (2, \"AI labs' share of NVIDIA's business\", 'roughly a quarter of NVIDIA’s business', 'guidance', 'NVIDIA')] LINK [{'question': 'clean-1', 'new_question': False, 'reason': \"States the scale of NVIDIA's equity investment in AI labs (nearly $50 billion) and management's expectation that balance-sheet-backed AI labs reach roughly a quarter of its business in fiscal 2028 — directly the investment-versus-support comparison the thesis tracks at the next annual report.\"}]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json;p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields/reader-packet-1-part3.json\\\"));[(print(\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"],a[\\\"publisher\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",a[\\\"text\\\"])) for a in p[\\\"articles\\\"] if a[\\\"article\\\"] in (8,10)]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json;p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields/reader-packet-1-part3.json\\\"));[(print(\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"],a[\\\"publisher\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",a[\\\"text\\\"])) for a in p[\\\"articles\\\"] if a[\\\"article\\\"] in (8,10)]'\"","aggregated_output":"ARTICLE 8 fool.com \n Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) , the world's most valuable company, is worth around $5.45 trillion as of this writing. The next big round number is $7 trillion. And the gap between them is larger than it sounds: roughly $1.55 trillion, or nearly the full market value of Broadcom ( AVGO +2.83% ) , the $1.69 trillion chip and infrastructure software business.\nWhat would have to be true of Nvidia's revenue, its margins, and its price-to-earnings multiple for the company to add almost a whole Broadcom to its value? And how long could it take?\nAfter all, fewer than 10 other companies in the world are worth more than the gap itself. At Nvidia's size, even a modest-sounding percentage gain today matches an entire giant company.\nStep back, though, and the climb looks smaller than the dollars suggest. From around $225 as I write this, a $7 trillion market cap comes out to around $290 a share -- a rise of around 28%.\nFor context, shares have climbed 37% from their 52-week low, and they now trade within around 5% of their 52-week high. I'd say nothing about a 28% move is unusual for this stock.\nNvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\n\"Now, compute is revenue,\" said CEO Jensen Huang in the company's second-quarter earnings release.\nThe forecasts go far past the quarter. Guidance for the fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue , around 12% above fiscal Q2. And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \"a supply-constrained outlook.\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.\nAnalysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion. Grow that by roughly 70%, and fiscal 2028 lands somewhere around $700 billion.\nMargins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\nWhen might it happen?\nNvidia's stock costs around 14 times forward earnings today, using analysts' fiscal 2028 estimates. At $290 per share, that forward price-to-earnings multiple would sit closer to 18 -- around what Broadcom gets today on the same basis.\nFiscal 2028 ends in January 2028, roughly 16 months from now. If Nvidia delivers the year it has forecast, and the market prices those earnings the way it prices Broadcom's today, $7 trillion arrives somewhere on that timeline.\nThe stock could also get there without any increase in its price-to-earnings multiple. Hold that multiple of expected earnings flat, and shares hit $290 when expectations for fiscal 2029 are around 28% over fiscal 2028's. Next to this fiscal year's expected 90% revenue growth and next year's forecast 70%, that would arguably be a low bar. But nothing about fiscal 2029 is guided yet. And estimates that far out can change a lot.\nOf course, the valuation multiple is also the half Nvidia doesn't control. Shares have risen roughly 21% in 2026 as of this writing, a year when quarterly profit more than doubled. That mismatch is the price-to-earnings multiple shrinking, quarter by quarter.\nIf the shrinking continues at anything like this year's pace, no forecast growth rate could take the stock to $7 trillion on this timeline.\nIn the end, what separates this milestone from past ones is that the growth to support it is already forecast. Reaching $7 trillion doesn't need a reacceleration or a richer valuation than Broadcom gets today.\nI think the earnings will show up more or less as forecast. What the market chooses to pay for them is another question.\nBy Daniel Sparks – Sep 25, 2026 at 7:24PM EST\nNvidia's market value is roughly $5.45 trillion as of this writing, leaving a gap of roughly $1.55 trillion to $7 trillion.\nARTICLE 10 finance.yahoo.com \n Shares of leading designer of graphics chips Nvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA) jumped 2.7% in the morning session after the company authorized an additional $150 billion for its share buyback program, taking the total authorization to $235 billion.\nAccording to CNBC, the chipmaker said the increase is the largest share-repurchase authorization boost in history and that it expects to complete the remaining buyback program through fiscal 2028. CEO Jensen Huang said in a statement that Nvidia's growth is being driven by a \"once-in-a-generation platform shift to AI and accelerated computing,\" and that strong cash generation lets the company invest in that buildout while returning capital to shareholders. Speaking on CNBC's \"Squawk Box,\" Huang called the AI infrastructure cycle the largest buildout in human history and said Nvidia plans to return more cash to shareholders as it generates it. A record buyback authorization typically supports the stock by signaling management confidence and reducing share count when cash flow is strong.\nAfter the initial pop, the shares cooled down to $231.14, up 2.7% from the previous close.\nNvidia's shares are not very volatile and have only had 8 moves greater than 5% over the last year. In that context, today's move indicates the market considers this news meaningful, although it might not be something that would fundamentally change its perception of the business.\nThe biggest move we wrote about over the last year was about 1 month ago when the stock gained 8.3% on the news that the company reported second-quarter financial results that topped Wall Street expectations and projected 70% revenue growth for the next year (fiscal 2028).\nAccording to a company press release and earnings commentary, Nvidia reported revenue of $96.22 billion for the second quarter, up 106% year-over-year, alongside adjusted earnings per share of $2.22. Both figures surpassed Wall Street consensus estimates, which called for revenue of $92.37 billion and adjusted earnings of $2.09 per share.\nNvidia is up 22.4% since the beginning of the year, and at $231.14 per share, it is trading close to its 52-week high of $235.74 from May 2026. Investors who bought $1,000 worth of Nvidia's shares 5 years ago would now be looking at an investment worth $11,167.\nALSO WORTH WATCHING: Nvidia's Quiet Partner. Nvidia's chips cost a hundred grand. The connectors that make them work cost even more. One company makes them all.\nEvery AI server needs specialized infrastructure the chip companies don't make. High-speed cables. Power connectors. Thermal sensors. This 90-year-old company built a monopoly on it. The AI boom just started. This stock is still flying under the radar. Claim The Stock Ticker Here for FREE .\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
